 "answer-767ed063-1f3b-5581-a169-3766dae98d5c"] ["answer-field-4b4192aa-858b-5977-ae07-cf74a154f636" :answer-field/choices "answer-02cb7704-5d65-5a8a-b6f4-5c1f2eca8d99"] ["question-776ef62d-2c09-5e2b-a7f2-e7cbc949af5b" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-4b4192aa-858b-5977-ae07-cf74a154f636"] [17592186133804 :knowledge-point/questions "question-776ef62d-2c09-5e2b-a7f2-e7cbc949af5b"] ["question-95655e19-4263-5e26-a218-4871ce1b1f2c" :question/id #uuid "95655e19-4263-5e26-a218-4871ce1b1f2c"] ["question-95655e19-4263-5e26-a218-4871ce1b1f2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-139645"] ["question-95655e19-4263-5e26-a218-4871ce1b1f2c" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-3$** | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $9$ | $6$ | $3$ | $-2$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{56}[\\begin{aligned}14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -28\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*"] ["question-95655e19-4263-5e26-a218-4871ce1b1f2c" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{56}[\\begin{aligned}14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -28\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $y=4-2x.$"] ["question-95655e19-4263-5e26-a218-4871ce1b1f2c" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-b80c480a-b5ef-5b14-b066-cb5d8f7268c9" :answer-field/id #uuid "b80c480a-b5ef-5b14-b066-cb5d8f7268c9"] ["answer-3414a2fb-1c40-5baf-b160-d62aa452c159" :answer/id #uuid "3414a2fb-1c40-5baf-b160-d62aa452c159"] ["answer-3414a2fb-1c40-5baf-b160-d62aa452c159" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-3414a2fb-1c40-5baf-b160-d62aa452c159" :answer/value "y=-2+4x"] ["answer-b301229a-92bc-5022-a48f-18260e3a02d2" :answer/id #uuid "b301229a-92bc-5022-a48f-18260e3a02d2"] ["answer-b301229a-92bc-5022-a48f-18260e3a02d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-b301229a-92bc-5022-a48f-18260e3a02d2" :answer/value "y=2+4x"] ["answer-580265ce-7676-517c-8e36-74524881eba6" :answer/id #uuid "580265ce-7676-517c-8e36-74524881eba6"] ["answer-580265ce-7676-517c-8e36-74524881eba6" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-580265ce-7676-517c-8e36-74524881eba6" :answer/value "y=2-4x"] ["answer-e41db3bb-d1d9-5070-ae1b-2af09a13aaa7" :answer/id #uuid "e41db3bb-d1d9-5070-ae1b-2af09a13aaa7"] ["answer-e41db3bb-d1d9-5070-ae1b-2af09a13aaa7" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-e41db3bb-d1d9-5070-ae1b-2af09a13aaa7" :answer/value "y=4+2x"] ["answer-6f203b32-367e-5763-8e6c-d666dfea11ee" :answer/id #uuid "6f203b32-367e-5763-8e6c-d666dfea11ee"] ["answer-6f203b32-367e-5763-8e6c-d666dfea11ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-6f203b32-367e-5763-8e6c-d666dfea11ee" :answer/value "y=4-2x"] ["answer-field-b80c480a-b5ef-5b14-b066-cb5d8f7268c9" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-b80c480a-b5ef-5b14-b066-cb5d8f7268c9" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-b80c480a-b5ef-5b14-b066-cb5d8f7268c9" :answer-field/correct "answer-6f203b32-367e-5763-8e6c-d666dfea11ee"] ["answer-field-b80c480a-b5ef-5b14-b066-cb5d8f7268c9" :answer-field/choices "answer-3414a2fb-1c40-5baf-b160-d62aa452c159"] ["answer-field-b80c480a-b5ef-5b14-b066-cb5d8f7268c9" :answer-field/choices "answer-b301229a-92bc-5022-a48f-18260e3a02d2"] ["answer-field-b80c480a-b5ef-5b14-b066-cb5d8f7268c9" :answer-field/choices "answer-580265ce-7676-517c-8e36-74524881eba6"] ["answer-field-b80c480a-b5ef-5b14-b066-cb5d8f7268c9" :answer-field/choices "answer-e41db3bb-d1d9-5070-ae1b-2af09a13aaa7"] ["answer-field-b80c480a-b5ef-5b14-b066-cb5d8f7268c9" :answer-field/choices "answer-6f203b32-367e-5763-8e6c-d666dfea11ee"] ["question-95655e19-4263-5e26-a218-4871ce1b1f2c" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-b80c480a-b5ef-5b14-b066-cb5d8f7268c9"] [17592186133804 :knowledge-point/questions "question-95655e19-4263-5e26-a218-4871ce1b1f2c"] ["question-d6dbf98c-84c4-5c67-a15a-4f405cbbd0b5" :question/id #uuid "d6dbf98c-84c4-5c67-a15a-4f405cbbd0b5"] ["question-d6dbf98c-84c4-5c67-a15a-4f405cbbd0b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-41401"] ["question-d6dbf98c-84c4-5c67-a15a-4f405cbbd0b5" :question/problem "The height (in feet) and the average number of points scored per game (APG) of four players on a basketball team are shown in the following table. \n\n| Height $(x)$ | **$6$** | **$6.5$** | **$6.5$** | **$7$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| APG $(y)$ | $5$ | $8$ | $10$ | $10$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the average number of points scored per game by a player whose height is $6.45\\,\\text{ft}?$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}84.75 & -13 \\\\ -13 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}33 \\\\ 217\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*"] ["question-d6dbf98c-84c4-5c67-a15a-4f405cbbd0b5" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 6.5 \\\\ 1 & 6.5 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}84.75 & -13 \\\\ -13 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}33 \\\\ 217\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-24.25 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=-24.25+5x.$ \n\nFinally, to predict the average number of points scored per game by a player whose height is $6.45\\,\\text{ft},$  we substitute $x=6.45$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-24.25+5(6.45) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model,  the average number of points scored per game by a player whose height is $6.45\\,\\text{ft}$ will be $8$  points per game."] ["question-d6dbf98c-84c4-5c67-a15a-4f405cbbd0b5" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard] ["answer-field-5784eef9-0ad2-5040-8dd4-135f8ec74797" :answer-field/id #uuid "5784eef9-0ad2-5040-8dd4-135f8ec74797"] ["answer-33db2298-5797-5cb1-af3d-bf806c19e083" :answer/id #uuid "33db2298-5797-5cb1-af3d-bf806c19e083"] ["answer-33db2298-5797-5cb1-af3d-bf806c19e083" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-33db2298-5797-5cb1-af3d-bf806c19e083" :answer/value "$7.75$  points per game"] ["answer-bd226916-bde7-517b-871e-7e8629dd5812" :answer/id #uuid "bd226916-bde7-517b-871e-7e8629dd5812"] ["answer-bd226916-bde7-517b-871e-7e8629dd5812" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-bd226916-bde7-517b-871e-7e8629dd5812" :answer/value "$8$  points per game"] ["answer-0334f116-416e-5d14-b3bf-b66d4f75de11" :answer/id #uuid "0334f116-416e-5d14-b3bf-b66d4f75de11"] ["answer-0334f116-416e-5d14-b3bf-b66d4f75de11" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-0334f116-416e-5d14-b3bf-b66d4f75de11" :answer/value "$8.25$  points per game"] ["answer-f5961ae6-fa3a-550a-9ffb-b71a8701df1f" :answer/id #uuid "f5961ae6-fa3a-550a-9ffb-b71a8701df1f"] ["answer-f5961ae6-fa3a-550a-9ffb-b71a8701df1f" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-f5961ae6-fa3a-550a-9ffb-b71a8701df1f" :answer/value "$8.75$  points per game"] ["answer-d2b10de6-86fa-53ca-94d7-3e008b6bfb17" :answer/id #uuid "d2b10de6-86fa-53ca-94d7-3e008b6bfb17"] ["answer-d2b10de6-86fa-53ca-94d7-3e008b6bfb17" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-d2b10de6-86fa-53ca-94d7-3e008b6bfb17" :answer/value "$9$  points per game"] ["answer-field-5784eef9-0ad2-5040-8dd4-135f8ec74797" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-5784eef9-0ad2-5040-8dd4-135f8ec74797" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-5784eef9-0ad2-5040-8dd4-135f8ec74797" :answer-field/correct "answer-bd226916-bde7-517b-871e-7e8629dd5812"] ["answer-field-5784eef9-0ad2-5040-8dd4-135f8ec74797" :answer-field/choices "answer-33db2298-5797-5cb1-af3d-bf806c19e083"] ["answer-field-5784eef9-0ad2-5040-8dd4-135f8ec74797" :answer-field/choices "answer-bd226916-bde7-517b-871e-7e8629dd5812"] ["answer-field-5784eef9-0ad2-5040-8dd4-135f8ec74797" :answer-field/choices "answer-0334f116-416e-5d14-b3bf-b66d4f75de11"] ["answer-field-5784eef9-0ad2-5040-8dd4-135f8ec74797" :answer-field/choices "answer-f5961ae6-fa3a-550a-9ffb-b71a8701df1f"] ["answer-field-5784eef9-0ad2-5040-8dd4-135f8ec74797" :answer-field/choices "answer-d2b10de6-86fa-53ca-94d7-3e008b6bfb17"] ["question-d6dbf98c-84c4-5c67-a15a-4f405cbbd0b5" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-5784eef9-0ad2-5040-8dd4-135f8ec74797"] [17592186133805 :knowledge-point/questions "question-d6dbf98c-84c4-5c67-a15a-4f405cbbd0b5"] ["question-1515bb33-4938-5f32-8710-f2dc1f06ddad" :question/id #uuid "1515bb33-4938-5f32-8710-f2dc1f06ddad"] ["question-1515bb33-4938-5f32-8710-f2dc1f06ddad" :question/math-academy-id "q-41739"] ["question-1515bb33-4938-5f32-8710-f2dc1f06ddad" :question/problem "A physics experiment consists of a small object moving along a straight line. The following table contains data collected on the distance traveled by the object (in inches) during the first five seconds. \n\n| Time $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Distance $(y)$ | $8.6$ | $13.1$ | $24.6$ | $28.1$ | $38.6$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what distance will the object cover after $6$ seconds, assuming the trend continues? \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}113 \\\\ 414\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*"] ["question-1515bb33-4938-5f32-8710-f2dc1f06ddad" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}8.6 \\\\ 13.1 \\\\ 24.6 \\\\ 28.1 \\\\ 38.6\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}113 \\\\ 414\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.1 \\\\ 7.5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=0.1+7.5x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the distance the object will cover after $6$ seconds, we substitute $x=6$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.1+7.5(6) \\\\  & =45.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the object will cover a distance of $45.1$ inches in $6$ seconds.  \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=6$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."] ["question-1515bb33-4938-5f32-8710-f2dc1f06ddad" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard] ["answer-field-7cbcd786-caa3-5353-bd0b-1dabaa09f399" :answer-field/id #uuid "7cbcd786-caa3-5353-bd0b-1dabaa09f399"] ["answer-0231280f-3c96-52d4-bb02-f31de91a72d7" :answer/id #uuid "0231280f-3c96-52d4-bb02-f31de91a72d7"] ["answer-0231280f-3c96-52d4-bb02-f31de91a72d7" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-0231280f-3c96-52d4-bb02-f31de91a72d7" :answer/value "$44.7$ inches"] ["answer-d868415f-f29e-51af-aefa-fa2b3c4c46cd" :answer/id #uuid "d868415f-f29e-51af-aefa-fa2b3c4c46cd"] ["answer-d868415f-f29e-51af-aefa-fa2b3c4c46cd" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-d868415f-f29e-51af-aefa-fa2b3c4c46cd" :answer/value "$45.1$  inches"] ["answer-2e066363-c73f-5f32-81f2-579f95f3d955" :answer/id #uuid "2e066363-c73f-5f32-81f2-579f95f3d955"] ["answer-2e066363-c73f-5f32-81f2-579f95f3d955" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-2e066363-c73f-5f32-81f2-579f95f3d955" :answer/value "$45.8$  inches"] ["answer-e78f56b5-9629-515c-a62c-674b58e812ce" :answer/id #uuid "e78f56b5-9629-515c-a62c-674b58e812ce"] ["answer-e78f56b5-9629-515c-a62c-674b58e812ce" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-e78f56b5-9629-515c-a62c-674b58e812ce" :answer/value "$46.2$  inches"] ["answer-54b64262-45e4-5112-8f5a-007f27ab1984" :answer/id #uuid "54b64262-45e4-5112-8f5a-007f27ab1984"] ["answer-54b64262-45e4-5112-8f5a-007f27ab1984" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-54b64262-45e4-5112-8f5a-007f27ab1984" :answer/value "$46.9$ inches"] ["answer-field-7cbcd786-caa3-5353-bd0b-1dabaa09f399" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-7cbcd786-caa3-5353-bd0b-1dabaa09f399" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-7cbcd786-caa3-5353-bd0b-1dabaa09f399" :answer-field/correct "answer-d868415f-f29e-51af-aefa-fa2b3c4c46cd"] ["answer-field-7cbcd786-caa3-5353-bd0b-1dabaa09f399" :answer-field/choices "answer-0231280f-3c96-52d4-bb02-f31de91a72d7"] ["answer-field-7cbcd786-caa3-5353-bd0b-1dabaa09f399" :answer-field/choices "answer-d868415f-f29e-51af-aefa-fa2b3c4c46cd"] ["answer-field-7cbcd786-caa3-5353-bd0b-1dabaa09f399" :answer-field/choices "answer-2e066363-c73f-5f32-81f2-579f95f3d955"] ["answer-field-7cbcd786-caa3-5353-bd0b-1dabaa09f399" :answer-field/choices "answer-e78f56b5-9629-515c-a62c-674b58e812ce"] ["answer-field-7cbcd786-caa3-5353-bd0b-1dabaa09f399" :answer-field/choices "answer-54b64262-45e4-5112-8f5a-007f27ab1984"] ["question-1515bb33-4938-5f32-8710-f2dc1f06ddad" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-7cbcd786-caa3-5353-bd0b-1dabaa09f399"] [17592186133805 :knowledge-point/questions "question-1515bb33-4938-5f32-8710-f2dc1f06ddad"] [17592186135062 :tutorial/content "Consider the following data set:\n\n| $x$ | **$100$** | **$110$** | **$120$** | **$130$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $12$ | $11$ | $12$ | $15$ |\n\nOur goal is to describe how to use matrices to carry out a linear regression. That is, we want to find an equation of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points. \n\n![](images/8e/8ec830e3cf6154675487536afc0e09a42226eb83187b90238a0bfdcefeae5b38.png)\n\nLet's write our problem in matrix notation. Substituting the values of $x$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+100{\\beta }_{1}=12 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+110{\\beta }_{1}=11 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+120{\\beta }_{1}=12 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+130{\\beta }_{1}=15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed in matrix notation as\n\n![](images/8b/8bacbea6990395351aecca8befe779358d57d50f8c14f865d73c2107688fa728.svg)\n\nThe matrix $X$ is called the **design matrix,** and $\\mathbf{y}$ is called the **observation vector**. \n\nSince our points do not lie on a straight line, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0$ and $\\beta_1$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0$ and $\\beta_1$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nThis approach to finding parameters for a linear regression model is often called **ordinary least squares (OLS) regression**.\n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta }=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0.1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line of best fit (regression line) is \n\n$$\ny = 1+ 0.1x.\n$$"] [17592186134029 :activity/expected-seconds 540.0]] "retractions" [[17592186135062 :tutorial/content "Consider the following data set:\n\n| $x$ | **$100$** | **$110$** | **$120$** | **$130$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $12$ | $11$ | $12$ | $15$ |\n\nOur goal is to describe how to use matrices to carry out a linear regression. That is, we want to find an equation of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points. \n\n![](images/8e/8ec830e3cf6154675487536afc0e09a42226eb83187b90238a0bfdcefeae5b38.png)\n\nLet's write our problem in matrix notation. Substituting the values of $x$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+100{\\beta }_{1} &=12 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+110{\\beta }_{1} &=11 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+120{\\beta }_{1} &=12 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+130{\\beta }_{1} &=15\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed in matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 100 \\\\ 1 & 110 \\\\ 1 & 120 \\\\ 1 & 130\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $X$ is called the **design matrix,** and $\\mathbf{y}$ is called the **observation vector**. \n\nSince our points do not lie on a straight line, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0$ and $\\beta_1$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0$ and $\\beta_1$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nThis approach to finding parameters for a linear regression model is often called **ordinary least squares (OLS) regression**.\n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta }=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0.1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line of best fit (regression line) is \n\n$$\ny = 1+ 0.1x.\n$$"]]}]